      "width": 2527,
      "height": 1951,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC",
      "description": "This true-color image of the Strait of Malacca was acquired on August 22, 2025, by the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) on NASA’s Aqua satellite.\nWhite “popcorn” clouds cover the forests of Malaysia (north) and Indonesia (south). These types of clouds are generally considered signs of fair weather and usually show up on sunny afternoons. They are formed when heat from the Sun warms the land surfaces in the area, which then warms the air directly above it. That warm air, and all of its cloud-making water vapor, then rises and condenses into clouds.\nPopcorn clouds don’t form over water, because bodies of water do not change temperature as rapidly as land, so water will remain cooler than land even during full Sun exposure. This means water doesn’t heat up enough to significantly warm the air above it, so the air doesn’t rise to form clouds.\n\nThe blue waters of the Strait of Malacca are stained in mud-colored tones and green. These colors mark pouring into the water from several rivers, especially from the Indonesian side of the Strait. As sediment floats near the surface, it appears tan or brown—very much like mud would look. As sediment sinks below the surface, reflectivity changes which means that the satellite sensor sees a shift in color. Sinking sediment typically looks green but as it gets deeper, sediment may even appear blue.",
